cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  cmdguard: checking a command sequence against the spacecraft state it will
  meet, with the state trace that reaches each violation
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-24
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes a declared command dictionary, a declared mode machine, a declared
  initial state and a candidate command sequence, and reports every violation
  together with the chain of earlier steps that produced the state it tripped
  over. Ten violation kinds are reported: undeclared command, mode gate,
  illegal mode transition, false precondition, unverified precondition,
  unverified precondition on an irreversible command, minimum same-command
  interval, minimum inter-command gap, mutual exclusion over a live hold, and a
  resource ceiling crossed by accumulation. Predicates are three-valued and an
  unverified predicate never satisfies a precondition, which is what makes the
  irreversible-command gate implementable: a sequence in which an irreversible
  command executes while one of its declared preconditions is unverified is
  refused, and the gate's clean verdict is named NO_REFUSAL_FOUND rather than
  anything that would read as a safety claim. Validation is level 2. Forward
  symbolic execution is exact for a single linear sequence against the declared
  model and is the ground truth; it is checked against hand-constructed cases
  whose expected violations and shortfalls are derived by hand, including a
  negative control of 420 generated clean sequences over seven generator shapes
  with zero false alarms. The per-command static precondition lint is measured
  against it on a seeded family of injected ordering defects: over 60 seeds and
  nine defect families, 1894 violations, the lint misses 0.524287 of them and
  0.667467 of the violations only a sequence-aware check can reach; with a
  timing pass added it misses 0.425026 and 0.441777. The lint also emits 17310
  findings against those 1894 violations, of which 0.937088 are not confirmed
  by the forward walk, because it re-evaluates every command occurrence against
  the declared initial state. A learned random-forest risk ranker over twenty
  static features triages long sequences for human review, scored on held-out
  seeds against the two free orderings the specification predicted would be
  hard to beat. It beats them: mean precision at 5 of 0.270199 against 0.156954
  for the irreversibility-flag ordering and 0.183444 for the resource-draw
  ordering, with paired bootstrap intervals excluding zero at every cut-off.
  Roughly a third of that advantage at k=5 is an artefact of the generator:
  withholding three timing-shape features drops the learned figure to 0.213907,
  because four of the nine defect families edit the time column. The free
  orderings themselves are not distinguished from a random shuffle at k=20. The
  ranker's reported dispersion is an ensemble spread and not a calibrated
  interval; the measured weighted mean absolute calibration gap is 0.331780.
  All learned results are statements about a seeded synthetic generator whose
  equations are its code; no flight data is used anywhere. No mature equivalent
  package was found on the index, and the honest substitute is mission-specific
  scripted checks plus review. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use, and a sequence this
  package reports clean has not been shown safe to uplink.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - command-sequence
  - spacecraft-operations
  - autonomous-aerospace-systems
  - symbolic-execution
  - mode-machine
  - constraint-checking
  - flight-operations
  - sequence-validation
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: King
        given-names: "J. C."
    title: "Symbolic execution and program testing"
    journal: "Communications of the ACM"
    volume: 19
    issue: 7
    year: 1976
    notes: >-
      The forward walk in cmdguard.trace is symbolic execution restricted to a
      single straight-line path, which is why it is exact rather than
      approximate for a linear command sequence. Structure only; no numerical
      value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Hopcroft
        given-names: "J. E."
      - family-names: Motwani
        given-names: "R."
      - family-names: Ullman
        given-names: "J. D."
    title: >-
      Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation
    edition: "3rd"
    publisher:
      name: "Pearson"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2, for the deterministic finite automaton the declared mode
      machine is. Structure only; no numerical value here is taken from it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Breiman
        given-names: "L."
    title: "Random forests"
    journal: "Machine Learning"
    volume: 45
    issue: 1
    year: 2001
    notes: The estimator behind cmdguard.ranker.RiskRanker.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wager
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Hastie
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Efron
        given-names: "B."
    title: >-
      Confidence intervals for random forests: the jackknife and the
      infinitesimal jackknife
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 15
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      Why the per-tree spread this package reports is a dispersion measure and
      not a confidence interval. The measured calibration gap of the risk
      itself is 0.331780.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: The random-forest implementation used by the risk ranker.
  - type: standard
    title: "Mission Operations -- Common Services"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 522.0-B-1"
    year: 2017
    notes: >-
      Cited only for the existence of a standardised notion of a commanding
      service with declared parameters and preconditions, which is why a
      declared command dictionary is a reasonable input format to assume. No
      numerical value and no schema in this repository is taken from it, and
      this package implements no CCSDS service.
